A||||6||PaUL||Door Hinge Pins
Z||000000||PaUL||07-30-99||07:20 PM||||Does anyone here know of an easy way to remove the lower door hinge pin? The upper pin is easy enough to remove using a socket extension and hammer. I couldnt figure out how to remove the lower one. Couldnt get the right angle to pound it out. 
Z||000001||Gokart||07-30-99||08:37 PM||||Try using your jack, thats how I did mine. Just raise the door from the bottom a little at a time while hitting the pin with a nail set and hammer. Got the new pins in mine, but didn't help much-still sags. I think it's because the hole itself has been through so many years of abuse, it's gotton too big for new pins to make a difference-good luck.
Z||000002||batboy||07-30-99||08:55 PM||||Most of the time when the hinge pins are shot, you need to buy the oversized bushing kit. Drill out the pin hole for the new bushing, then install the new bushing and matching pin.
Z||000003||ChadMan||08-02-99||07:56 AM||||Didn't somebody have details on this in another thread?  I think they said you can get replacement door pins and oversized bushings from the HELP! line of products.  I can't seem to find the thread right now - I have a saggy driver's door too.
Z||000004||batboy||08-02-99||05:37 PM||||The post you're looking for is called "Rattling door" and this is what Ogre said about those replacement hinge pins:<p>There is a kit in the red "help!" blister packs available from many parts dealers. There are 2 kits, with bushings & new pins, for GM. you want the shorter ones. You need on kit per hinge, and 2 kits are less than 10$us.
Z||000005||theogre||08-03-99||07:50 AM||||Look on the pins..... there are some small spring tabs that hold the pin in. break them off, and the pin should be easier to remove.
Z||000006||PaUL||08-04-99||07:52 PM||||breaking those pins off makes it easier but not easy.
